a new year for many of us means it's  time to make resolutions but sticking  with them can be a challenge 2018 study  found only six percent of Americans  fully stocked to their resolutions New  York Times bestselling author James  clear offers a roadmap to lifestyle  change in his book atomic habits an easy  and proven way to build good habits and  break bad ones he's here to help us make  resolutions that will last  James clear good morning and happy New  Year to you good morning let me start  with the key what is the key to making a  good New Year's resolution well most of  the time when people make a new year's  resolution they're essentially making a  goal for themselves right the thought is  okay I want different outcomes this year  I want to lose weight or I want to  meditate more reduce stress I want to be  more productive but I think actually the  typical New Year's resolution focuses on  the wrong thing what I mean by that is  we think the outcome is the thing that  needs to change but actually it's the  habits or the system the process behind  the outcome so for example if you have a  messy room you look around the room and  you think oh this is so cluttered I need  a clean room but if you get motivated  and clean the room for an hour you have  a clean room for now but if you don't  change the sloppy messy packrat habits  that led to a dirty room in the first  place then you end up in the same place  and it's similar with New Year's  resolutions we think what I need is to  feel less stress or what I need is to  have a written book or what I need is to  lose weight but actually what we really  need are the habits and the systems  behind those things yes I won't ask you  about to have a written book which is a  little too personal to me but if it's  clean the room let's say or lose weight  how then do I wire my life with good  habits so I think the first thing to  focus on is not the outcome that you  want but the type of person you want to  become so if you take losing weight a  lot of people may say oh I want to lose  40 pounds or something like that but the  question asked is who is the type of  person that could lose weight well maybe  it's the type of person who doesn't miss  workouts and so your new year's  resolution becomes not to lose a certain  amount of weight or to achieve a  particular goal but to be a certain type  of person and this is where I think  small habits are so useful because even  if doing something like 5 push-ups is  not enough to transform your body  overnight it is enough  cast a vote for being that kind of  person for being a fit person and so in  a sense it's like every action you take  as a vote for the kind of person you  believe that you are and so the first  task is to start casting votes for that  new identity and then worry about  ramping up the intensity later and is  the key word there action you're you're  giving yourself an action item whereas  clean the room or be a clean person or  be a thin person those are gargantuan  big things certain actions so you  translate that identity that you're  looking to build into a small action a  small vote that you can take so you know  things like doing five push-ups writing  one sentence meditating for sixty  seconds these don't sound like ambitious  New Year's resolutions but all of them  cast a vote for being a fit person or  becoming a writer or being a meditator  and ultimately when you get to the end  of the year what you want it's not to  have a particular result but to look at  yourself in a new way all right it will  vote not just in November but with our  actions James clear thanks so much thank  you
 

